  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 121 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAs indicated above, widespread rain chances will continue through \nThursday night, especially south of an Ogallala through \nO'Neill line. Southwest flow aloft will continue to bring \nsub-tropical moisture northeastward across southwest into \neastern NE. Surface low pressure should deepen from east \ncentral CO into northwest KS. A weak frontal boundary may extend\neast of the surface low to near the NE/KS border. There remains\nsome disagreement between guidance on the location of this \nboundary. The NAM is farthest north, the GFS farthest south, \nwhile the RRFS is middle ground near the NE/KS border. This will\naffect areas which see the highest rainfall amounts Thursday \nnight into Friday morning.\n\nPrecipitation chances from the NBM on Friday remain likely \nacross north central and central NE while ended across \nsouthwestern areas. Do feel increasing westerlies from the \nnorthern stream should push precipitation chances east through \nthe day, with some breaks of sun in the west by afternoon. Highs\nto reach the mid 70s far southwest, with low 70s most \nlocations, and the coolest mid 60s in the northeast. Still low \nchances for showers and a few thunderstorms southeast Friday \nevening. Upper ridging will progress eastward across the region \nSaturday and Sunday bringing in noticeably warmer highs from 75\nto 85 each day along with dry conditions and mainly sunny \nskies.\n\nAn upper trough over south central Canada and an upper trough \nextending back across the western U.S may set up a frontal \nboundary across South Dakota Sunday night, with a cold front \npushing southeast through KS into IA on Tuesday. This will bring\nincreasing POPS for showers and storms Sunday night into Monday,\nwith likely POPS Monday night and chance POPS Tuesday. Highs in\nthe 70s Monday will cool to the 60s to near 70 Tuesday.",
